D. Cross-lane Tournament Analysis
  lane=tournament_decks metric=(5, 2, 5, 5) note=raw vertex-decks collide through n=6, but the H/c3/deleted-score loss lift resolves all checked buckets
  lane=union_closed_frequency metric=(5, 3, 4, 5) note=Frankl holds through m=4 but frequency vectors are visibly lossy
  lane=finite_field_incidence metric=(4, 4, 5, 4) note=Kakeya line-choice carriers expose multiplicity and distance side channels
  tournament={'top_order': ['union_closed_frequency', 'tournament_decks', 'finite_field_incidence'], 'score_hist': {0: 1, 1: 1, 2: 1}, 'directed_3cycles': 0, 'hamiltonian_paths': 1, 'edges': [('tournament_decks', 'finite_field_incidence'), ('union_closed_frequency', 'tournament_decks'), ('union_closed_frequency', 'finite_field_incidence')]}

E. Practical synthesis
  Reconstruction: raw vertex-decks and H/score/c3/SCC scalars collide
  through n=6; scalar card-decks help, and the H/c3/deleted-score loss
  profile resolves every checked collision.  That loss profile is the
  first side-channel lift to test before broader graph decks.
  Union-closed: the half-frequency witness is easy at m<=4, but sorted
  frequency vectors forget many closure systems; set-pressure is a cheap
  reattachment channel.
  Finite-field incidence: line-choice Kakeya sets give a small exact
  direction carrier where multiplicity profiles and distance fibers can be
  tracked together before touching Euclidean Kakeya/Falconer questions.
